<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Byelorussian SSR</h1><p class="st-meta">1920 CE – 1991 CE · capital Minsk</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Soviet Belarus, devastated in the Second World War and a founding member of the United Nations, which became the independent Republic of Belarus in 1991.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minsk">Minsk</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in 1920, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byelorussian-ssr">Byelorussian Soviet Socialist Republic</a> was proclaimed again on 31 July 1920, replacing the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lithuanian-byelorussian-ssr">Lithuanian-Byelorussian SSR</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-riga">Peace of Riga</a> in 1921 divided Belarusian lands between it and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/second-polish-republic">Second Polish Republic</a>, and in 1922 it became a founding member of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The 1920s brought Belarusian-language schools and culture, followed by collectivisation and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Stalin</a>'s purges, whose victims lie at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurapaty">Kurapaty</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minsk">Minsk</a>. The republic absorbed western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a> in 1939. German occupation from 1941 to 1944 killed perhaps a quarter of the population and destroyed hundreds of villages, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khatyn-massacre">Khatyn</a>. It gained a seat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> in 1945 and was industrialised under leaders such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyotr-masherov">Pyotr Masherov</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Heavily affected by fallout from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chernobyl-disaster">Chernobyl</a> in 1986, the republic declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> in 1990 and independence on 25 August 1991. Renamed the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a>, it signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belovezha-accords">Belovezha Accords</a> dissolving the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in December 1991.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/byelorussian-ssr.jpg" alt="Byelorussian SSR" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Zscout370, reworked by Pianist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Byelorussian_Soviet_Socialist_Republic_(1951%E2%80%931991).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1920 CE – 1991 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Minsk</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Kirill Mazurov, Pyotr Masherov, Stanislav Shushkevich</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>State atheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Belarusian, Russian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/belarus">Belarus</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Byelorussian Soviet Socialist Republic, Soviet Belarus</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1956&amp;focus=byelorussian-ssr" aria-label="Byelorussian SSR on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5291 327 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Byelorussian SSR lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5611 583L5556 562M5611 583L5820 533"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5556 562h0M5820 533h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5611 583h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5611 583h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Minsk. <a href="/atlas?year=1956&amp;focus=byelorussian-ssr">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
